#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055968 :tutorial/content "Multiplying a purely imaginary number by a real number is similar to multiplying two monomials.\n\nFor example, we can multiply the purely imaginary number $3 \\textrm{i}$ by the real number $6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 3\\text{i} & =(6\\cdot 3)\\text{i}=18\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we can multiply a complex number by a real number using the distributive property.\n\nFor example, we can multiply the complex number $2-3\\textrm{i}$ by the real number $3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n3(2-3\\textrm{i}) &= 3\\cdot2 + 3\\cdot(-3\\textrm{i}) \\[3pt]\n\t\t&= 6 + 3\\cdot (-3)\\textrm{i} \\[3pt]\n\t\t&= 6 -9\\textrm{i}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4e54cae3-6a6c-50bb-a0d7-5ddc1fefaaf2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2789 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055972 :tutorial/content "Finally, we can multiply two complex numbers just like multiplying two binomials. Again, we use the fact that $\\mathrm{i}^2=-1.$\n\nSo, to multiply $2 + \\textrm{i}$ and $5 + 3\\textrm{i},$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(2+\\textrm{i})(5+3\\textrm{i}) &= 2 (5+3\\textrm{i})  + \\textrm{i} (5+3\\textrm{i}) \\\n\t\t &= 2\\cdot5 + 2\\cdot(3\\textrm{i}) +\\textrm{i}\\cdot 5+ \\textrm{i} \\cdot (3\\textrm{i}) \\\n\t\t&= 10 + 6\\textrm{i} + 5\\textrm{i} +3\\textrm{i}^2 \\\n\t\t&= 10 + 11\\textrm{i} + 3(-1) \\\n\t\t&= 10 + 11\\textrm{i}  - 3 \\\n\t\t&= 7 + 11\\textrm{i}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c3f115ff-3eaa-551b-b31d-11d45b3c2c0d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2856 :tutorial/title "Multiplying Two Complex Numbers"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055973 :tutorial/content "Multiplying two purely imaginary numbers is similar to multiplying two monomials. But we also need to use the fact that $\\textrm{i}^2=-1.$\n\nFor example, to multiply $2\\textrm{i}$ and $3\\textrm{i},$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}(2\\text{i})(3\\text{i}) & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =6{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =6(-1) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKnowing this, we can also multiply a complex number by a purely imaginary number.\nFor instance, to multiply $2\\textrm{i}$ and $4-3\\textrm{i},$ we use the distributive property and the fact that $\\textrm{i}^2=-1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2\\textrm{i}(4-3\\textrm{i})\n\t\t\t&= 2\\textrm{i}\\cdot 4+2\\textrm{i}\\cdot(-3\\textrm{i}) \\[3pt]\n&=2\\cdot 4\\textrm{i}+2\\cdot(-3)\\textrm{i}^2\\[3pt]\n&= 8\\textrm{i}+(-6)(-1)\\[3pt]\n&= 8\\textrm{i}+6\\[3pt]\n\n&=6+8\\textrm{i}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f770f0f-47fd-5f77-b4f3-e19b7dd9e29e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2857 :tutorial/title "Multiplying by a Purely Imaginary Number"}]}
